  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The recommended solution is to <b>deploy the new application component as an Elastic Beanstalk application, collect replies via the APIs, store the records in DynamoDB, and use Apache Hive with Amazon EMR for analytics</b>. This design offers the following key advantages:</p><ul><li><b>Durability and Accessibility</b>: DynamoDB is a fully managed, serverless NoSQL database with high durability and immediate accessibility, ideal for real-time and historical data storage for analytics.</li><li><b>Scalable Analytics</b>: Amazon EMR and Apache Hive can efficiently analyze vast amounts of structured and semi-structured data at any time, leveraging the data stored in DynamoDB.</li><li><b>Seamless Integration</b>: Elastic Beanstalk simplifies deployment and scaling for your Ruby application, while DynamoDB and EMR integrate naturally with other AWS analytics tools.</li></ul><p>Why the other options are unsuitable:</p><ul><li><i>\"Set up the new component in an Auto Scaling group of Amazon EC2 instances to retrieve the social replies, save the data on Amazon EBS volumes, and utilize AWS Data Pipeline to send it to Amazon Kinesis for analysis.\"</i> <br>Amazon EBS volumes are attached to specific EC2 instances and are not inherently durable or accessible outside those instances. Storing analytics data on EBS is not recommended for long-term, highly available, or scalable analytics workflows.</li><li><i>\"Deploy the new component as an Amazon Elastic Beanstalk application, retrieve replies from the social network, store replies on Amazon EBS, and stream the results into Amazon CloudWatch for analysis using Amazon Kinesis.\"</i> <br>This process uses EBS again (which is not recommended for long-term storage), and Amazon CloudWatch is primarily designed for metrics and logs, not large-scale historical data analytics.</li><li><i>\"Launch the new component on an Auto Scaling group of EC2 instances, extract replies from social media, archive the data in Amazon Glacier, and use AWS Data Pipeline to transmit it to Amazon Redshift for analytical purposes.\"</i> <br>Amazon Glacier is designed for cold/archival storage, making it unsuitable for applications requiring frequent and low-latency access to recent and historical analytics data.</li></ul><p><b>Tips & Insights:</b> Always pair highly durable, low-latency data stores (like DynamoDB, S3, or Redshift) with analytics tools for business intelligence use cases—avoid EBS and Glacier for such data. Use managed services like Elastic Beanstalk for simplified deployments and scaling.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You have a Ruby-based application running on AWS Elastic Beanstalk that posts marketing messages to social networking platforms. Management now requests that all responses and replies to these posts be systematically captured, so that the results of your current and previous campaigns can be compared and analyzed over time. The new component you created is able to fetch these replies from social media APIs. <br><br>What is the best approach for reliably storing these social media responses to ensure they remain accessible and can be used for analytics, both now and in the future?</p>",
